WEBVTT

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.245
Performance tuning begins with a repeatable workload.

00:00:03.245 --> 00:00:07.285
Record the model, quantization, prompt, context setting, runtime version,

00:00:07.285 --> 00:00:08.756
and acceleration backend.

00:00:08.756 --> 00:00:12.410
Then measure time to first token, prompt processing speed, generation tokens per second,

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:13.905
and total completion time.

00:00:13.905 --> 00:00:16.185
Tokens per second describes generation throughput,

00:00:16.185 --> 00:00:19.240
but it does not capture a long delay while the prompt is processed.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:23.480
Both measurements affect whether a coding assistant or chat interface feels responsive.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:27.320
Watch GPU utilization and VRAM allocation while the request runs.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:31.210
Low GPU activity may indicate CPU execution, insufficient offload,

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:33.385
or a workload that is waiting elsewhere.

00:00:33.385 --> 00:00:38.195
VRAM near its limit can produce allocation failures or force compromises in context size.

00:00:38.195 --> 00:00:40.520
Monitor system RAM and storage activity as well;

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:44.680
heavy swapping can make a configuration technically functional but operationally unusable.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:47.700
Thermals, fan noise, and power draw matter during sustained use.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:51.520
Observe temperatures and clock speeds over a long session, not only during startup.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:55.160
Falling clocks combined with rising temperature indicate thermal throttling.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:58.665
Sudden shutdowns or instability may point to power, cabling, overclocking,

00:00:58.665 --> 00:01:00.174
or cooling problems.

00:01:00.174 --> 00:01:04.070
Prefer a stable power profile over a marginal performance gain that increases heat and

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:05.385
noise substantially.

00:01:05.385 --> 00:01:06.654
Change one setting at a time.

00:01:06.654 --> 00:01:08.740
GPU offload, batch size, context length,

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:12.900
and runtime-specific cache options can improve one phase while worsening another or

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.240
increasing memory use.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:17.640
Keep a small benchmark table and compare results against the real workload.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.955
The objective is not the highest isolated number.

00:01:19.955 --> 00:01:22.690
It is predictable latency, adequate output quality,

00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:26.320
and sustained operation within the machine's thermal and memory limits.
